# acme-not-a-joke

A Haskell library of primitives to perform ACME certifications
([RFC 8555](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8555)): the protocol
by which a provider such as Let's Encrypt issues TLS certificates.

The package is named `acme-not-a-joke` because `acme-` packages on Hackage
typically are "joke" packages. This one is not.

```hs
import Acme.NotAJoke.Client
import Acme.NotAJoke.Api.Directory
import Acme.NotAJoke.KeyManagement
import Acme.NotAJoke.LetsEncrypt
import Data.Maybe

loadedjwk <- loadJWKFile "staging/key.jwk"
let jwk = fromJust loadedjwk
let contacts = ["mailto:certmaster@dicioccio.fr"]

leDir <- fetchDirectory (directory staging_letsencryptv2)
nonce0 <- fromJust <$> getNonce leDir.newNonce
postCreateAccount jwk leDir.newAccount nonce0 (createAccount contacts)
```

The library is incomplete and subject to changes, but the happy path works:
create an account, place an order, answer a challenge, and fetch the
certificate.

The code is on GitHub at
[`lucasdicioccio/acme-not-a-joke`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/acme-not-a-joke).

## Explore

- [**Introducing acme-not-a-joke**](/introducing-acme-not-a-joke.html): what
  the library covers today, how its types are organised, and how to get a
  first certificate from the Let's Encrypt staging environment.

